Oxygen, redox and the changing forms of constituents (@wu-redox: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @water-and-sanitation, #source "../sources/Basic-Principles-of-Wastewater-Treatment.lingua#r_083WF4FQEZQHB29YJBHWVZ0BEV", #source "../sources/Biological-Wastewater-Treatment-Chen-and-Colleagues.lingua#r_1AE9DK0YWQR5GDECZ9KK1JJP37", #source "../sources/Industrial-Water-Treatment-UFC-3-230-13.lingua#r_41KNKBCA6GZWQ6VAJGAPG2HJ3R", #water-and-sanitation "Treatment-barriers-and-the-evidence-for-their-performance.lingua#r_4KN934MCH4BZF0JMKMYN0VYRRB") { r_7SJ9QMVSF9TWJG7Z36EZ8R1YC4
**Read:** [Basic Principles of Wastewater Treatment — von Sperling](../sources/Basic-Principles-of-Wastewater-Treatment.lingua#r_083WF4FQEZQHB29YJBHWVZ0BEV), oxygen transfer and biological-conversion foundations; [Biological Wastewater Treatment — Chen and colleagues, second edition](../sources/Biological-Wastewater-Treatment-Chen-and-Colleagues.lingua#r_1AE9DK0YWQR5GDECZ9KK1JJP37), microbiology, metabolism and nutrient-conversion chapters; [Industrial Water Treatment Operation and Maintenance — UFC 3-230-13](../sources/Industrial-Water-Treatment-UFC-3-230-13.lingua#r_41KNKBCA6GZWQ6VAJGAPG2HJ3R), makeup-water and corrosion mechanisms.

Aeration, oxygen consumption, biological conversion and mineral reactions can change both a constituent's form and its location. Dissolved iron can become a removable solid under suitable conditions; reduced sulfur or nitrogen compounds have different reactions and oxygen demands from their oxidized forms. These changes require a species and electron account.

Distinguish dissolved oxygen from an oxidation–reduction potential reading. A single electrode potential in a mixed, reacting water does not establish equilibrium among every redox couple. Temperature, gas exchange, kinetics, surfaces and sample handling can all affect the observation.

Follow one conversion through reactants, products, alkalinity and gas or solid outputs. Compare the theoretically required electron acceptor with actual transfer and competing demand. Explain why a chemically possible conversion may be slow, incomplete or coupled to a biological community under the operating conditions.

